= Morning observations

The canopy first becomes visible as a broken line of green against the sky. At
the north ridge the upper canopy closes before the creek bend receives direct
light, so the two plots are not directly comparable without a cover estimate.

== Light and leaf area

#table(
  columns: (1fr, auto, auto),
  inset: 7pt,
  stroke: 0.5pt + luma(185),
  table.header([Plot], [Canopy cover], [Light]),
  [North ridge], [78%], [high],
  [Creek bend], [64%], [medium],
  [Old trail], [52%], [medium],
)

The canopy cover values are approximate. Record the observer and the time of day
beside each estimate, because a low sun angle can make an open canopy look
denser than it is.

== A second pass

The second pass should revisit the same canopy gaps rather than choosing new
ones. A repeatable sample is more useful than a larger but inconsistent one.
